<body>

    <header>
        <h1>The Sunk Cost Fallacy</h1>
        <p>Why we continue bad investments, broken projects, and bad habits—and how to stop doing it.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="container">
        
        <!-- What is it section -->
        <section>
            <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1454165804606-c3d57bc86b40?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1000&q=80" alt="Decision making and analysis" class="section-image">
            <h2>What is the Sunk Cost Fallacy?</h2>
            <p>The <strong>Sunk Cost Fallacy</strong> is a cognitive bias where a person continues a behavior, endeavor, or investment as a result of previously invested resources (time, money, or effort).</p>
            <div class="highlight-box">
                <p><strong>The Core Mistake:</strong> Making decisions based on what you've already lost or spent, rather than looking strictly at future costs and potential rewards.</p>
            </div>
            <p>Because we deeply dislike the feeling of losing or wasting things (Loss Aversion), we throw good money after bad, or waste more time on dead ends, hoping to justify our past choices.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- Examples section -->
        <section>
            <h2>Real-World Examples</h2>
            <p>This psychological trap manifests itself in various areas of life:</p>
            <div class="examples-grid">
                
                <div class="example-card">
                    <div>
                        <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1489599849927-2ee91cede3ba?auto=format&fit=crop&w=600&q=80" alt="Cinema theater seats">
                        <h3>1. The Bad Movie Ticket</h3>
                        <p>You buy a $20 ticket. Twenty minutes in, you realize the movie is terrible. The fallacy tells you to stay so your money "isn't wasted." The rational choice? Walk out and save your time.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="example-card">
                    <div>
                        <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1507679799987-c73779587ccf?auto=format&fit=crop&w=600&q=80" alt="Business planning">
                        <h3>2. Failing Projects</h3>
                        <p>A company pours millions into outdated software. Instead of canceling, leadership invests more because "we've already spent so much and cannot quit now."</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="example-card">
                    <div>
                        <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1516589178581-6cd7833ae3b2?auto=format&fit=crop&w=600&q=80" alt="Couple walking together">
                        <h3>3. Toxic Relationships</h3>
                        <p>Staying in an unhappy relationship solely because of "all the years we've already invested together," ignoring whether the future holds any happiness.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- How to avoid section -->
        <section>
            <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1517245386807-bb43f82c33c4?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1000&q=80" alt="Team strategy planning" class="section-image">
            <h2>How to Avoid the Fallacy</h2>
            <p>Overcoming this mental trap requires shifting your focus from the past to the future. Here are practical ways to beat it:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Ignore Past Investments:</strong> Pretend you are starting fresh today with zero history attached. Ask yourself: <em>"Would I start this project right now if I hadn't invested anything before?"</em></li>
                <li><strong>Define Exit Strategies Early:</strong> Before entering a major project, set clear objective boundaries (like a budget or timeline) where you will re-evaluate or walk away.</li>
                <li><strong>Seek an Outside Perspective:</strong> Talk to a trusted friend or colleague who has no emotional attachment to what you've already spent.</li>
                <li><strong>Accept Losses as Tuition:</strong> Reframe past losses not as "waste," but as tuition paid for a valuable lesson.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

    </div>

    <footer>
        <p>&copy; 2026 Sunk Cost Fallacy Guide. Built for smart decision-making.</p>
    </footer>

</body>
